# =============================================================================
# Aquarius Keys — letting the logged-in person's own programs read the keyboard
# =============================================================================
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
#
# udev is the part of Linux that notices hardware and decides who is allowed to
# use it. This file adds two permissions, and nothing else.
#
# The keyboard remapper that gives AquariusOS its Mac-style shortcuts needs to
# do two things that ordinary programs are not allowed to do:
#
#   1. READ THE KEYBOARD DIRECTLY, at /dev/input/event*, so it can see the keys
#      you actually pressed before any app does.
#   2. INVENT A KEYBOARD, through /dev/uinput, so it can type the replacement
#      keys as if they had come from real hardware.
#
# Out of the box Linux reserves both for the administrator, so the remapper
# would have to run as root. Running a program that watches every key you type
# as the administrator, for the whole life of the machine, is exactly the thing
# not to do. This file is how it runs as you instead.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `uaccess` — WHY THIS, AND NOT A GROUP
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There are two ways to grant this, and the difference matters.
#
#   THE COMMON WAY, which we did NOT use: put the person in a group called
#   "input". It works, and it is what xremap's own instructions say. But the
#   permission is then PERMANENT and UNCONDITIONAL: that account can read the
#   keyboard whether it is logged in or not, from an SSH connection, while the
#   screen is locked, forever. And on an image-based system like AquariusOS
#   there is no good moment to add an existing account to a group — the
#   installer has already created it by the time our image is on disk.
#
#   WHAT WE DO INSTEAD: `TAG+="uaccess"`. This is systemd's own mechanism for
#   "give this to whoever is physically logged in at this screen, right now".
#   The permission is granted at login and TAKEN AWAY AT LOGOUT, automatically,
#   by the same part of Linux that already does this for your webcam, your
#   microphone and your graphics card. Nobody is added to any group; a second
#   user who logs in gets it and the first one loses it; an SSH connection
#   never gets it at all.
#
#   It is a strictly smaller permission than the group, granted for a strictly
#   shorter time, with nothing to undo if the feature is removed.
#
# ⚠️ THE HONEST CAVEAT, WRITTEN DOWN BECAUSE IT IS REAL. Whichever way you do
# it, the person sitting at the machine can now read every key typed on it.
# That is unavoidable — it is what remapping keys *is* — and it is the same
# exposure every "Mac keys on Linux" tool has. On a single-person creator
# machine it is the right trade. On a shared server it would not be.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY 70-, AND WHY /usr/lib RATHER THAN /etc
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# udev reads its rule files in number order and the tagging rules systemd ships
# are numbered 70-, so ours sits alongside them rather than before the devices
# it is talking about exist. Rules that belong to the operating system live in
# /usr/lib/udev/rules.d; /etc/udev/rules.d is for a person's local changes, and
# putting ours there would mean an update could not fix a mistake in it.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The real keyboards.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One /dev/input/event* file per input device — keyboard, mouse, lid switch,
# power button. systemd already hands out game controllers this way; keyboards
# it does not, because most programs have no business reading them. Ours does.
SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event[0-9]*", TAG+="uaccess"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. The invented keyboard.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /dev/uinput is the door a program goes through to create a keyboard of its
# own. Three things are set here and each one earns its place:
#
#   TAG+="uaccess"              the same "whoever is at the screen" grant
#   MODE="0660", GROUP="input"  a second, independent path to the same
#                               permission. If `uaccess` ever fails on a
#                               machine — it depends on a login session being
#                               registered correctly — a person can fall back
#                               to `sudo usermod -aG input $USER` and it works
#                               immediately, with no image change. This line is
#                               what makes that fallback possible.
#   OPTIONS+="static_node"      /dev/uinput does not exist until the uinput
#                               driver is loaded, and a device that does not
#                               exist cannot have permissions. This tells udev
#                               to apply them to the node the moment it appears
#                               — which is what makes the FIRST login after a
#                               fresh install work rather than the second.
KERNEL=="uinput", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", TAG+="uaccess", MODE="0660", GROUP="input", OPTIONS+="static_node=uinput"
